{
  "findings": [
    {
      "title": "Inconsistent sample size: text says 105 patients but Table 3 lists 105 patients with ORR values that do not match the number of responders (e.g., 21/105=20.0% is correct, but 24/105=22.9% is correct, but 27/105=25.7% is correct, 31/105=29.5% is correct, 36/105=34.3% is correct; however, the precision values are inconsistent with the CI widths: for 20.0% CI (12.4,27.7) width is 15.3, half-width 7.65, but they report 7.7% (rounding), for 22.9% CI (14.8,30.9) half-width 8.05, they report 8.0% (rounding), for 25.7% CI (17.4,34.1) half-width 8.35, they report 8.4% (rounding), for 29.5% CI (20.8,38.3) half-width 8.75, they report 8.8% (rounding), for 34.3% CI (25.2,43.4) half-width 9.1, they report 9.1% (exact). These are minor rounding inconsistencies, but the main issue is that the text says 'If observed ORR ranges from 20.0% to 34.3%, the precision estimation using 105 patients ranges from 7.7% to 9.1%.' That is consistent. However, the sample size justification is based on a single-arm study with no formal hypothesis, but the primary endpoint is ORR, and the sample size is not justified with a target ORR or a null hypothesis. This is a missing justification.",
      "page": 76,
      "quote": "The planned total sample size for this study is 105 patients. ... If observed ORR ranges from 20.0% to 34.3%, the precision estimation using 105 patients ranges from 7.7% to 9.1%.",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inconsistent definition of on-treatment period: 'day from first dose' should be 'date' or 'day after'? Also, the on-treatment period is defined as 'the day from first dose of study drug to the last dose of study drug plus 90 days, or 1 day prior to starting a new treatment for cervical cancer, whichever is first.' This is ambiguous and potentially contradictory: 'whichever is first' could mean the earlier of the two, but the on-treatment period should end at the earlier of last dose +90 days or 1 day prior to new treatment. However, the phrase 'whichever is first' is unclear because it could be interpreted as the first event, but the on-treatment period should end at the earlier of the two, which is correct. But the definition of 'day from first dose' is not standard; it should be 'date of first dose' or 'day of first dose'. This is a minor wording issue.",
      "page": 78,
      "quote": "The on-treatment period is defined as the day from first dose of study drug to the last dose of study drug plus 90 days, or 1 day prior to starting a new treatment for cervical cancer, whichever is first.",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inconsistent definition of posttreatment period: 'until 1 year after the end of the on-treatment period or the start of a new treatment for cervical cancer.' This is ambiguous: does it mean the posttreatment period ends at the earlier of 1 year after on-treatment end or start of new treatment? The phrase 'or' is ambiguous; it should specify 'whichever occurs first'.",
      "page": 78,
      "quote": "The posttreatment period is defined as the day after the on-treatment period until 1 year after the end of the on-treatment period or the start of a new treatment for cervical cancer.",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inconsistent definition of treatment-emergent adverse events: TEAEs are defined as those that are not present at baseline or exacerbation of pre-existing condition during the on-treatment period. However, the on-treatment period includes 90 days after last dose, which is standard. But the definition of 'on-treatment period' includes '1 day prior to starting a new treatment for cervical cancer' which is odd because it would end the on-treatment period before the new treatment starts, but the TEAE definition says 'during the on-treatment period' which is fine. No contradiction.",
      "page": 78,
      "quote": "Treatment-emergent adverse events are defined as those that are not present at baseline or represent the exacerbation of a pre-existing condition during the on-treatment period.",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inconsistent statement: 'This is a single arm, open label study and there is no formal statistical hypothesis in this study.' But later in Section 11.4.4 it says 'This a single arm, open label, and observational study.' The study is not observational; it is an interventional trial. This is a contradiction.",
      "page": 76,
      "quote": "This is a single arm, open label study and there is no formal statistical hypothesis in this study.",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inconsistent statement: 'This a single arm, open label, and observational study.' The study is not observational; it is an interventional trial. This is a contradiction.",
      "page": 78,
      "quote": "This a single arm, open label, and observational study.",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inconsistent statement: 'There will be no formal interim analysis in this study.' But earlier in Section 11.4.3 it says 'In order to describe ORR, the interim data cutoff for efficacy analysis will be after the 53rd patient enrolled has had an opportunity for 4 tumor assessments.' This is an interim analysis, even if not formal. Contradiction.",
      "page": 77,
      "quote": "In order to describe ORR, the interim data cutoff for efficacy analysis will be after the 53rd patient enrolled has had an opportunity for 4 tumor assessments.",
      "severity": "critical"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inconsistent statement: 'There will be no formal interim analysis in this study.' But earlier in Section 11.4.3 it says 'In order to describe ORR, the interim data cutoff for efficacy analysis will be after the 53rd patient enrolled has had an opportunity for 4 tumor assessments.' This is an interim analysis, even if not formal. Contradiction.",
      "page": 80,
      "quote": "There will be no formal interim analysis in this study.",
      "severity": "critical"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inconsistent statement: 'The primary efficacy analysis will be performed when the last patient enrolled has had the opportunity for at least 4 tumor assessments.' But the final data cutoff for efficacy analysis is also when the last patient enrolled has had the opportunity for 4 tumor assessments. This is consistent, but the primary analysis is the same as the final analysis, which is fine. However, the interim data cutoff is after the 53rd patient, but there is no formal interim analysis, so the interim data cutoff is not used for any formal analysis. This is a contradiction.",
      "page": 77,
      "quote": "The final data cutoff for efficacy analysis will be when the last patient enrolled has had the opportunity for 4 tumor assessments.",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inconsistent statement: 'The primary efficacy analysis will be performed when the last patient enrolled has had the opportunity for at least 4 tumor assessments.' But the final data cutoff for efficacy analysis is also when the last patient enrolled has had the opportunity for 4 tumor assessments. This is consistent, but the interim data cutoff is after the 53rd patient, but there is no formal interim analysis, so the interim data cutoff is not used for any formal analysis. This is a contradiction.",
      "page": 77,
      "quote": "Primary efficacy analysis will be performed when the last patient enrolled has had the opportunity for at least 4 tumor assessments.",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inconsistent statement: 'The primary endpoint for efficacy analyses is the investigator-assessed ORR as determined by RECIST version 1.1 (Eisenhauer, 2009).' But the study is single-arm, and the primary objective is to estimate clinical benefit, but there is no formal hypothesis. The sample size justification is based on precision, but no target ORR is specified. This is a missing justification.",
      "page": 77,
      "quote": "The primary endpoint for efficacy analyses is the investigator-assessed ORR as determined by RECIST version 1.1 (Eisenhauer, 2009).",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inconsistent statement: 'The ORR is defined as the proportion of patients who achieve a best overall response (BOR) of CR or PR. Patients who are not evaluable for BOR will be considered as non-responders.' This is standard, but the analysis of ORR uses a normal approximation of the binomial distribution, which is not appropriate for small sample sizes or when the number of responders is small. However, with 105 patients, it is acceptable. No issue.",
      "page": 77,
      "quote": "The ORR along with the 2-sided 95% confidence interval using a normal approximation of the binomial distribution will be summarized.",
      "severity": "minor"
    }
  ]
}
